              COMPLETE GIT COMMAND CHEAT SHEET
============================================================


========================
1. GIT SETUP
========================

git --version
Check installed Git version.

git config --global user.name "Your Name"
Set your Git username globally.

git config --global user.email "your@email.com"
Set your Git email globally.

git config --global --list
Display global Git configuration.

git config user.name "Your Name"
Set username only for the current repository.

git config user.email "your@email.com"
Set email only for the current repository.


========================
2. CREATE / CLONE REPOSITORY
========================

git init
Initialize a new Git repository in the current folder.

git clone <repository-url>
Clone an existing remote repository.

git clone <repository-url> <folder-name>
Clone a repository into a specified folder.

cd <folder-name>
Enter the cloned/project directory.


========================
3. REMOTE REPOSITORIES
========================

git remote -v
Display configured remote repository URLs.

git remote add origin <repository-url>
Add a remote repository named origin.

git remote set-url origin <repository-url>
Change the URL of the origin remote.

git remote remove origin
Remove the origin remote.

git remote show origin
Display detailed information about origin.

git push -u origin main
Push main to origin and set upstream tracking.

git push origin main
Push the local main branch to origin.

git push
Push the current branch to its configured remote.


========================
4. STATUS / CHANGES
========================

git status
Show staged, unstaged and untracked files.

git diff
Show unstaged changes.

git diff --cached
Show staged changes.

git diff --staged
Show staged changes.

git diff main...feature
Compare changes between main and feature branches.

git diff main...feature -- <file>
Compare a specific file between two branches.


========================
5. ADD / COMMIT
========================

git add <file>
Stage a specific file.

git add .
Stage all changes in the current directory.

git add -A
Stage all changes including additions and deletions.

git commit -m "message"
Create a commit with the specified message.

git commit --amend
Modify the most recent commit.

git commit --amend --no-edit
Add changes to the latest commit without changing its message.

git commit --amend -m "New message"
Change the latest commit message.


========================
6. BRANCHES
========================

git branch
List local branches.

git branch -a
List local and remote branches.

git branch -r
List remote branches.

git branch --show-current
Show the currently active branch.

git branch <branch-name>
Create a new branch.

git switch <branch-name>
Switch to an existing branch.

git switch -c <branch-name>
Create and switch to a new branch.

git checkout <branch-name>
Switch to an existing branch.

git checkout -b <branch-name>
Create and switch to a new branch.

git branch -d <branch-name>
Delete a merged local branch.

git branch -D <branch-name>
Force-delete a local branch.


========================
7. COMMON FEATURE BRANCHES
========================

git switch -c feature/player-registration
Create and switch to player-registration feature branch.

git switch -c feature/course-recommendation
Create and switch to course-recommendation feature branch.

git switch -c feature/registration
Create and switch to registration feature branch.

git switch -c feature/event-registration
Create and switch to event-registration feature branch.


========================
8. FETCH / PULL
========================

git fetch
Download remote changes without integrating them.

git fetch origin
Download updates from the origin remote.

git pull
Fetch and integrate changes from the tracked remote branch.

git pull origin main
Fetch and integrate the remote main branch.

git pull --rebase origin main
Fetch and rebase the current branch onto remote main.


========================
9. MERGE
========================

git merge <branch>
Merge another branch into the current branch.

git merge origin/main
Merge the latest remote main into the current branch.

git switch main
Switch to the main branch.

git merge feature/player-registration
Merge the feature branch into main.

git merge --abort
Cancel an in-progress merge.


========================
10. MERGE CONFLICT
========================

git status
Show files involved in a merge conflict.

git add <resolved-file>
Mark a manually resolved conflict as resolved.

git commit
Complete the merge after resolving conflicts.

git merge --continue
Continue a merge after resolving conflicts when supported.

git merge --abort
Cancel the merge and return to the previous state.


========================
11. REBASE
========================

git fetch origin
Download the latest remote changes before rebasing.

git rebase origin/main
Replay current branch commits on top of latest main.

git rebase main
Replay current branch commits on top of local main.

git rebase --continue
Continue a rebase after resolving a conflict.

git rebase --abort
Cancel the rebase and restore the previous state.

git push --force-with-lease
Safely update a remote branch after rewriting its history.


========================
12. UNDO / RESTORE
========================

git restore <file>
Discard uncommitted changes to a file.

git restore .
Discard all uncommitted working-directory changes.

git restore --staged <file>
Unstage a file while keeping its changes.

git restore --staged .
Unstage all staged files.

git reset --soft HEAD~1
Remove the latest commit while keeping changes staged.

git reset HEAD~1
Remove the latest commit while keeping changes unstaged.

git reset --hard HEAD~1
Remove the latest commit and discard its changes.

git revert <commit-id>
Create a new commit that reverses an existing commit.


========================
13. DELETE / UNTRACK FILE
========================

git rm <file>
Delete a file and stage the deletion.

git rm --cached <file>
Stop tracking a file while keeping it locally.

git rm -r --cached .
Remove all files from tracking while keeping them locally.


========================
14. .gitignore
========================

echo "db-config.env" >> .gitignore
Add db-config.env to .gitignore.

git add .gitignore
Stage the .gitignore file.

git commit -m "Update gitignore"
Commit .gitignore changes.


========================
15. STASH
========================

git stash
Temporarily save uncommitted changes.

git stash push -m "message"
Save changes with a descriptive stash message.

git stash list
List all saved stashes.

git stash pop
Restore the latest stash and remove it from the stash list.

git stash apply
Restore the latest stash but keep it in the stash list.

git stash apply stash@{0}
Apply a specific stash.

git stash drop
Delete the latest stash.

git stash clear
Delete all stashes.


========================
16. PATCH FILES
========================

git diff > changes.patch
Create a patch containing unstaged changes.

git diff --cached > changes.patch
Create a patch containing staged changes.

git diff -- <file> > changes.patch
Create a patch for a specific file.

git apply --check changes.patch
Check whether a patch can be applied without applying it.

git apply changes.patch
Apply a patch.

git apply --reject --whitespace=fix changes.patch
Apply a patch while handling whitespace and rejected hunks.

git apply --3way changes.patch
Try a three-way merge when applying a patch.


========================
17. FORMAT-PATCH
========================

git format-patch -1 HEAD
Create a patch file from the latest commit.

git format-patch main..feature
Create patch files for commits on feature not in main.


========================
18. LOG / HISTORY
========================

git log
Display commit history.

git log --oneline
Display compact commit history.

git log --oneline -5
Display the latest five commits.

git log --oneline --graph --decorate --all
Display a compact visual branch/merge history.

git log main..feature
Show commits present in feature but not main.

git log feature..main
Show commits present in main but not feature.

git show <commit-id>
Display details of a specific commit.


========================
19. TAGS
========================

git tag
List all tags.

git tag v1.0
Create tag v1.0.

git tag -a v1.0 -m "Version 1.0"
Create an annotated tag.

git push origin v1.0
Push a specific tag to GitHub.

git push origin --tags
Push all local tags to GitHub.


========================
20. REMOTE BRANCH TRACKING
========================

git branch -vv
Show local branches and their upstream tracking branches.

git push -u origin feature/player-registration
Push a feature branch and establish upstream tracking.

git branch --set-upstream-to=origin/main main
Set origin/main as the upstream for local main.


========================
21. FORK / UPSTREAM
========================

git clone <your-fork-url>
Clone your forked repository.

git remote add upstream <original-repository-url>
Add the original repository as upstream.

git remote -v
Verify origin and upstream remotes.

git fetch upstream
Download updates from the original repository.

git switch main
Switch to your main branch.

git merge upstream/main
Merge the original repository's main into your main.

git push origin main
Push the updated main to your fork.

git switch -c feature/my-feature
Create a feature branch.

git push -u origin feature/my-feature
Push the feature branch to your fork for a pull request.


========================
22. SSH AUTHENTICATION
========================

ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "your@email.com"
Generate an SSH key pair.

eval "$(ssh-agent -s)"
Start the SSH agent in Git Bash.

ssh-add ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
Add the private SSH key to the SSH agent.

cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub
Display the public SSH key to add to GitHub.

ssh -T git@github.com
Test GitHub SSH authentication.

git remote set-url origin git@github.com:USERNAME/REPOSITORY.git
Change the repository remote from HTTPS to SSH.


========================
23. GITHUB PUSH
========================

git add .
Stage all project changes.

git commit -m "Update project"
Commit the staged changes.

git push origin main
Push main to GitHub.

git push -u origin feature/branch-name
Push a new feature branch and track it remotely.


